.TH FCGI_Accept 3 1997-09-16 "Open Market, Inc." "FastCGI Developer's Kit Library Manual"
.SH NAME
FCGI_Accept, FCGI_ToFILE, FCGI_ToFcgiStream - fcgi_stdio compatibility library

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "fcgi_stdio.h"

.I int
.br
\%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR\fI(void)\fR;

.I FILE\ *
.br
\%\fBFCGI_ToFILE\fR\fI(FCGI_FILE *)\fR;

.I FCGI_Stream\ *
.br
\%\fBFCGI_ToFcgiStream\fR\fI(FCGI_FILE *)\fR;

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR() function accepts a new request from the HTTP server
and creates a CGI-compatible execution environment for the request.

If the application was invoked as a CGI program, the first call to
\%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR() is essentially a no-op and the second call returns \-1.
This causes a correctly coded FastCGI Responder application
to run a single request and exit, giving CGI behavior.

If the application was invoked as a FastCGI server, the first call to
\%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR() indicates that the application has completed its
initialization and is ready to accept its first request.
.
Subsequent calls to \%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR() indicate that the application has
completed processing its current request and is ready to accept a new request.
.
An application can complete the current request without accepting
a new one by calling \%\fBFCGI_Finish\fR(3); later, when ready to accept a new
request, the application calls \%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR().

In completing the current request, \%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR() may detect errors,
e.g. a broken pipe to a client who has disconnected early.
\%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR() ignores such errors.
.
An application that wishes to handle such errors should explicitly call
\%\fBfclose\fR(stderr), then \%\fBfclose\fR(stdout); an \%\fBEOF\fR return from
either one indicates an error.

If the environment variable \%\fBFCGI_WEB_SERVER_ADDRS\fR is set when
\%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR() is called, it should contain a comma-separated
list of IP addresses.  Each IP address is written as four decimal
numbers in the range [0..255] separated by decimal points.
(\%\fBnslookup\fR(8) translates the more familiar symbolic IP hostname
into this form.)  So one legal binding for this variable is

.EX
FCGI_WEB_SERVER_ADDRS=199.170.183.28,199.170.183.71
.EE

\%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR() checks the peer IP address of each new connection
for membership in the list.
If the check fails (including the possibility that the connection didn't
use TCP/IP transport), \%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR() closes the connection
and accepts another one (without returning in between).

After accepting a new request, \%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR() assigns new values
to the global variables \%\fBstdin\fR, \%\fBstdout\fR, \%\fBstderr\fR,
and \%\fBenviron\fR. After \%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR() returns, these variables
have the same interpretation as on entry to a CGI program.

\%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR() frees any storage allocated by the previous call to it.
This has important consequences:

.IP
DO NOT retain pointers to the environ array or any strings
contained in it (e.g. to the result of calling \%\fBgetenv\fR(3)),
since these will be freed by the next call to \%\fBFCGI_Finish\fR() or
\%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR().

.IP
DO NOT use \%\fBsetenv\fR(3) or \%\fBputenv\fR(3) to modify the environ
array created by \%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR(), since this will either leak
storage or cause the next call to \%\fBFCGI_Finish\fR() or
\%\fBFCGI_Accept\fR() to free storage that should not be freed.

.IP
If your application needs to use \%\fBsetenv\fR(3) or \%\fBputenv\fR(3)
to modify the environ array, it should follow this coding pattern:

.EX
	char **savedEnviron, **requestEnviron;
	int acceptStatus;

	savedEnviron = environ;
	acceptStatus = FCGI_Accept();
	requestEnviron = environ;
	environ = savedEnviron;
	if(acceptStatus >= 0 && !FCGX_IsCGI()) {
		/*
		* requestEnviron points to name-value pairs in
		* storage allocated by FCGI_Accept.  OK to read,
		* not OK to retain pointers -- make copies instead.
		*/
	}
	/*
	* OK to do setenv or putenv, but beware of storage leaks!
	*/
.EE

.PP
In addition to the standard CGI environment variables, the environment
variable \%\fBFCGI_ROLE\fR is always set to the role of the current
request. The roles currently defined are \%\fBRESPONDER\fR,
\%\fBAUTHORIZER\fR, and \%\fBFILTER\fR.

In the \%\fBFILTER\fR role, the additional variables
\%\fBFCGI_DATA_LENGTH\fR and \%\fBFCGI_DATA_LAST_MOD\fR are also defined.
See the manpage \%\fBFCGI_StartFilterData\fR(3) for complete information.

The macros \%\fBFCGI_ToFILE\fR and \%\fBFCGI_ToFcgiStream\fR are provided
to allow escape to native functions that use the types \%\fBFILE\fR or
\%\fBFCGI_Stream\fR.  In the case of \%\fBFILE\fR, functions would have to
be separately compiled, since \%\fBfcgi_stdio.h\fR replaces the standard
\%\fBFILE\fR with \%\fBFCGI_FILE\fR.

.SH RETURN VALUES
0 for successful call, \-1 for error (application should exit).

